\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11c41377-dcf0-57a2-b4f3-049053a2f2ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512872 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512887 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512887 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512887 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512887 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & 15 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512887 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52c2be5d-881c-537a-9b60-cd461ebff8ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512887 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{al